  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper-level low over the Pacific Northwest is continuing to \ndig southward into the western US. This setup has allowed for\nPacific moisture to be transported into Montana. Combined with a \nsurface cold front that moved through the region, some showers are\nalready occuring and should persist through Monday.\n\nForecast QPF totals for the next couple of days have trended \nhigher compared to previous model guidance. Much of the region \ncould see as much as half an inch to an inch of total \nprecipitation with lower amounts in far southeastern zones. \nInitially much of the precipitation will fall as rain. Rain will \nthen mix with and change to snow tonight over the Little Rockies \nas temperatures fall while wet snow will be possible Sunday \nevening through Monday morning elsewhere across the CWA. \nAccumulating snow should remain more concentrated in northwestern \ncounties along the immediate Canadian border and the higher peaks \nof the Little Rockies.\n\nAccumulations overall should remain very little, if any, as warm \nground temperatures will prevent snowfall from sticking to the \nground with the exception of the Little Rockies and higher \nelevations along the Canadian border. Terrain-enhanced lift may \ncause localized heavy snowfall at times, producing locally higher\namounts of up to a few inches of snowfall accumulation in some \nareas described. As a result, a Winter Weather Advisory has been\nissued for Southwest Phillips County, though a wide range in\nsnowfall totals is expected depending on elevation.\n\nTemperatures for Sunday and Monday will be much cooler than \naverage due to cold Canadian air lingering along the backside of \nthe cold front. Beyond Monday, temperatures should gradually warm\nup as an upper- level cutoff low develops over the Four Corners \nregion and advects warmer air into the CWA.\n\nModel agreement deteriorates significantly beyond \nWednesday, so confidence remains low for weather conditions for the\nend of the week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n\nConfidence of below average temperatures through Monday are high\nbut decreases as the week progresses and becomes low by the end \nof the week. Precipitation chances later in the week depends on \nthe track of the low pressure system over the Four Corners. \n\nWhile confidence in QPF amounts have trended higher, precipitation\ntype, timing, and snow accumulation remain below average. Temperatures\ncould trend cooler than forecasted due to evaporative cooling \neffects, however, warm ground temperatures still remain and will\nprevent much accumulating snow from sticking with the exception of\nthe Little Rockies. Temperatures will continue to be monitored \nfor changes in winter weather impacts. \n\nKENZ",
